export const ONLINE_ENGLISH_FEEDBACK_SKILL: string = "---\nname: online-english-feedback\ndescription: \"Generate a parent/student-facing daily feedback report and an English-language tutor evaluation report from an online (video) English tutoring class transcript. Use whenever the user provides a class transcript (STT output) and asks to \\\"make feedback,\\\" \\\"피드백 리포트 작성해줘,\\\" \\\"수업 리포트 작성해줘,\\\" \\\"QC 리포트,\\\" \\\"강사 평가,\\\" or similar — even a short \\\"피드백\\\"/\\\"리포트\\\" with a pasted transcript. User can optionally state learner type (\\\"kid,\\\" \\\"adult,\\\" \\\"teen,\\\" \\\"아이,\\\" \\\"성인\\\") to skip auto-detection. The student-facing report is written in the student's native language (Korean, Japanese, Chinese, Vietnamese, or Thai) — if nationality isn't stated, ask before writing rather than guessing. Always produces TWO outputs: a warm, tone-adapted report (child/teen → parent-addressed, adult → direct), messenger-ready for KakaoTalk/WeChat; plus a professional English tutor evaluation. Length scales with class duration. Trigger proactively whenever a tutor pastes a lesson transcript.\"\n---\n\n# Online English Class Feedback & Tutor Evaluation Report Generator\n\nTurns a raw class transcript (tutor + student, English tutoring, already\nspeech-to-text transcribed) into two very differently-toned deliverables.\nGetting the **tone split right, the age-appropriate addressing right, and\nscaling to class length right** are the most important parts of this\nskill — read this whole file before writing anything.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Read the full transcript first.** Identify: main topic(s) covered,\n   notable vocabulary/expressions the tutor introduced, grammar or\n   pronunciation mistakes the student made (and how the tutor corrected\n   them), how much each person spoke, any awkward silences/interruptions,\n   and anything the student said about their interests, frustrations, or\n   requests. STT output is often messy — filter out clearly-garbled\n   repeated fragments (e.g. long runs of \"Okay.\" \"Yes.\" \"Bye.\" from\n   audio dropouts) rather than treating them as real dialogue content.\n2. **Determine the learner profile** — if the user stated it explicitly\n   (kid/teen/adult), use that. Otherwise infer it (see \"Detecting\n   Learner Profile\" below). This determines who Output 1 is addressed to\n   and its tone.\n3. **Determine Output 1's language** — if the user stated the student's\n   nationality/language when submitting the transcript, use it (see\n   \"Detecting Report Language\" below). If not stated, this is NOT\n   something to infer or default silently — ask the user which language\n   to write Output 1 in before producing the reports (a transcript of an\n   English lesson gives no reliable signal of the student's nationality).\n4. **Detect class duration** from the transcript's timestamps (last\n   timestamp ≈ total length) — this determines how much content Output 1\n   should include (see \"Scaling to Class Length\" below).\n5. **If other info is missing** (e.g., no date, unclear topic, tutor\n   name not stated), don't stop and ask — make a reasonable inference\n   from context (e.g. use today's date, infer topic from the material\n   covered) and proceed. Only ask the user if the transcript is too\n   short/garbled to produce a real report, or if the learner-profile\n   signal is genuinely too ambiguous to guess (rare) — this is separate\n   from the language question in step 3, which is always asked when\n   unstated.\n6. Write **Output 1: Feedback Report** (in the determined language, plain\n   text, tone/addressee per learner profile — see below).\n7. Write **Output 2: Tutor Evaluation Report** (always in English,\n   regardless of Output 1's language — plain text, see below).\n8. Present both as two clearly separated plain-text blocks in the chat\n   (not as files/artifacts) so the user can copy-paste each block\n   directly into KakaoTalk/WeChat without editing it first. Do NOT add\n   your own wrapper labels like \"📩 학부모/수강생 전달용\" or \"Output 1:\"\n   above each block — those aren't meant to be sent and would have to be\n   deleted before pasting. Instead, just separate the two blocks with a\n   blank line (or a plain \"---\" divider) so it's visually clear where\n   one ends and the other begins, without adding text the user has to\n   strip out.\n\n## Detecting Report Language\n\nOutput 1 (the student/parent-facing report) is written in the language\nthat matches the student's nationality. Supported: 한국어 (Korean),\n日本語 (Japanese), 中文 (Chinese), Tiếng Việt (Vietnamese), ภาษาไทย\n(Thai).\n\n- **If the user states the nationality/language** when submitting the\n  transcript (e.g. \"일본 학생,\" \"Vietnamese student,\" \"중국인 성인반\"),\n  write the entire Output 1 report in that language — section labels,\n  the summary, the vocab comments, the grammar explanations, and the\n  closing note all go in that language. Keep the actual English lesson\n  content in English where it's the subject being taught (the target\n  vocab word itself, example sentences, the student's ❌/✅ quoted\n  sentences) — translate the surrounding explanation and commentary, not\n  the English being taught.\n- **If the user doesn't state it, ask** — don't guess from the\n  transcript and don't silently default to Korean. A short question is\n  enough, e.g. \"이 학생 국적이 어디인가요? (한국어로 작성할지, 다른 언어로\n  작성할지 확인하려고요)\"\n- This is a **per-transcript** setting, not a standing preference —\n  ask again each time it isn't stated, since a tutor may handle students\n  of different nationalities across different transcripts in the same\n  conversation.\n- When translating, write naturally in the target language at the same\n  level of professionalism and analytical depth as the Korean version\n  (see \"Output 1: Feedback Report\" below) — this is a fresh report\n  written in that language, not a literal/mechanical translation.\n- Output 2 (the tutor evaluation) is always written in English\n  regardless of Output 1's language, since it's addressed to the tutor.\n\n## Detecting Learner Profile\n\n**If the user explicitly states the learner type** (e.g. \"kid,\" \"adult,\"\n\"teen,\" \"아이,\" \"성인,\" \"학생\") when submitting the transcript, use that\ndirectly — skip inference entirely and go straight to the matching\naddressing style below.\n\nOtherwise, infer from transcript content — don't ask unless truly\nambiguous:\n\n- **Child (elementary-age)**: textbook units with simple picture-based\n  vocabulary (school rooms, animals, colors), references to \"mommy/daddy,\"\n  siblings, playing, very short/simple sentences, tutor uses lots of\n  praise and playful language, tutor may address a parent for logistics.\n  → Output 1 is addressed **to the parent, about the child** (3rd person:\n  \"OO(이)가 오늘...\").\n- **Student (older child / teenager)**: school-subject vocabulary,\n  homework, exams, more complex grammar points, still clearly a minor.\n  → Output 1 is still addressed **to the parent**, but tone shifts from\n  \"cute/playful\" to \"achievement/progress\"-oriented — less baby-talk,\n  more like a academic progress note.\n- **Adult**: workplace, travel, news, personal opinions, abstract topics,\n  self-directed learning goals, no parent/guardian references.\n  → Output 1 is addressed **directly to the learner**, professional-warm,\n  second person (\"~님\").\n\nIf signals conflict or it's genuinely unclear, default to treating the\nlearner as the primary audience (adult-style, direct address) and briefly\nflag the assumption to the user in your reply (not inside the report\nitself).\n\n## Detecting Class Duration\n\nUse the last timestamp in the transcript. ~20-30 min → treat as a\n**25-minute class**. ~45-55 min → treat as a **50-minute class**.\nAnything clearly in between, use judgment and scale proportionally.\n\n## Formatting rules (both outputs)\n\nSince these get pasted into KakaoTalk/WeChat, **do not use markdown**:\n- No `**bold**`, no `#` headers, no markdown tables.\n- Use plain line breaks, simple dashes ( - ) or bullet dots ( • ) for\n  lists, and emojis as section markers instead of headers (Output 1 only\n  — see emoji rule per output below).\n- Keep line lengths short/readable on a phone screen.\n- Use a title line instead of a markdown header.\n\n## Output 1: Feedback Report (in the student's language — see \"Detecting Report Language\" above)\n\nTone: warm but professional — this reads as a **tutor's progress note to an\nadult** (the parent, or the adult learner themselves), never as baby-talk\nor as if the words are being spoken to the child directly. Even for a\nyoung child's report, keep the register that of a teacher briefing a\nparent: specific, factual, respectful — not cutesy, not over-exclaimed,\nnot sing-song. The reader should finish this and feel like the tutor\nreally paid individual attention and is speaking to them as a fellow\nadult, not that they're reading a children's-book voice.\nReference specific things the student actually said or did. Avoid\ngeneric praise (\"good job!\") without a specific moment backing it up,\nand avoid stacking exclamation points — one per report at most, if any.\n\nEmojis: light use as section markers/accents only (😊 ✅ 📘 💬 🌟) — not in\nevery line, not more than one per line, and never used as filler\npunctuation to soften a childish tone.\n\n### Avoid AI-sounding stock phrases\n\nThis is the single most important stylistic rule. A parent/student who\nreads the same opening or closing line every day will immediately clock\nit as a template, which kills the \"personally cared for\" feeling this\nreport exists to create. So:\n\n- **Never reuse a fixed opening or closing sentence.** Do not default to\n  phrases like \"오늘 수업은 이런 시간이었어요,\" \"정말 대견한 성장이에요,\"\n  \"오늘도 열심히 참여했어요,\" or similar boilerplate — these read as\n  AI-generated regardless of how true they are. Every report's opening\n  and closing lines should be freshly written from what's actually in\n  *this* transcript, and should sound different from report to report.\n- Start the summary with a concrete, specific detail from the class\n  (something the student said, a moment they got something right after\n  struggling, a topic they got excited about) rather than a generic\n  scene-setting sentence.\n- The summary should give a genuine sense of **the whole class**, not\n  just one anecdote. Cover, briefly: what the lesson's main topic/goal\n  was, what the student actually practiced or produced (cite a specific\n  phrase or moment), and how they did overall — not only the single most\n  charming moment. A parent should be able to picture what happened for\n  the full 25 (or 50) minutes, not just the opening two minutes of\n  small talk. For a 25-min class this usually takes 3-4 sentences; for a\n  50-min class, more (see length note in that structure below).\n- Write the way a real tutor jotting a quick note to a parent would —\n  slightly informal, specific, sometimes a little uneven — not like a\n  press release. Short, plain sentences beat polished, symmetrical ones.\n- Vary sentence openers and structure across the report (don't start\n  every bullet's blurb the same way, don't end every report on the same\n  rhythm of \"앞으로도 ~해봐요!\").\n- If you notice yourself writing something that could paste unchanged\n  into a completely different student's report, rewrite it to be\n  specific to this one.\n\n### Handling attitude/behavior issues (optional section)\n\nIf the transcript shows the student genuinely not following\ninstructions, being distracted/uncooperative, or otherwise showing poor\nclassroom attitude, include a short, gentle note about it so the parent\nhas it for reference — but the goal is \"useful heads-up,\" never\n\"complaint\" or \"blame.\" Only include this when there's real evidence in\nthe transcript; don't invent or exaggerate it, and don't include it for\nminor, normal-kid fidgeting that isn't actually a pattern.\n\n- Only relevant for **child/teen** profiles (parent-addressed reports).\n  For **adult** learners, skip this section entirely — if focus/effort\n  came up for an adult, that's between the tutor and the note isn't\n  needed in a report addressed to the learner themselves.\n- Frame it gently and constructively, never as a complaint about the\n  child: describe the specific moment factually, then pivot to something\n  forward-looking or a small suggestion, rather than dwelling on it.\n  E.g. \"오늘은 중간에 살짝 집중이 흐트러지는 순간이 있었어요 — 다음 시간엔\n  ~해보면 좋을 것 같아요\" rather than \"지안이가 선생님 말을 잘 안 들었어요.\"\n- Keep it to 1-2 sentences, placed near the end (right before or folded\n  into 선생님의 한마디), so the report still closes on a warm, forward-\n  looking note rather than ending on the criticism.\n- Never use this section to pile on multiple issues — if there were\n  several, pick the one most worth a parent knowing about.\n\n### Addressing by learner profile\n\n- **Child** → write in 3rd person about the child, addressed to the\n  parent, in the register of a teacher's progress note — not baby-talk\n  and not written as if speaking to the child. E.g. \"지안이가 오늘 ~를\n  학습했습니다,\" \"지안이는 스스로 문장을 구성해냈습니다.\" Warm and specific,\n  but restrained and professional — no baby-talk vocabulary, no stacked\n  exclamation points, no sing-song phrasing.\n- **Student (teen)** → still addressed to the parent, 3rd person, in the\n  same professional progress-note register: \"OO 학생이 오늘 ~를 학습했습니다,\"\n  achievement-framed, can mention study habits or areas to reinforce at\n  home.\n- **Adult** → addressed directly to the learner, 2nd person (\"~님\"),\n  professional-warm, no parent framing at all.\n\n### Structure — 25-minute class (plain text, no markdown)\n\n```\n📘 [Date] 오늘의 수업 피드백 — [Topic]\n\n[Freshly-written 3-4 sentence summary — open with a specific detail from\n this class (not a generic scene-setter), then cover the lesson's main\n topic/goal and what the student actually practiced, so the reader gets\n a real sense of the whole class, not just one moment. Addressed per\n learner profile.]\n\n📝 오늘의 학습 내용\n- [Activity/segment 1 — one line, e.g. \"Unit 핵심 어휘 4개 학습 및 문장 만들기\"]\n- [Activity/segment 2 — one line]\n(2-3 lines — the specific things actually done in class, not a generic\n \"vocabulary + reading\" label)\n\n💬 오늘 배운 주요 표현\n- [Word/Phrase] — [meaning]\n  예문: [student's own example sentence, or the tutor's model if the\n  student didn't produce one]\n  [One specific line on how well the student understood/used it — not\n  filler praise. E.g. \"새로운 단어를 정확하게 이해하고 자연스럽게\n  사용했습니다,\" or \"의미는 잘 전달됐지만 문장 구조는 다음에 한 번 더\n  연습하면 좋겠습니다.\" Vary this per item — don't praise every single\n  one identically.]\n(3-4 items)\n\n✅ 조금만 다듬으면 더 좋아지는 부분\n[One framing sentence normalizing these as a natural part of learning —\n vary the wording each time, don't reuse a fixed line.]\n\n① [short label for the error type] : [original] → [corrected]\n❌ [student's original sentence, verbatim from transcript]\n✅ [corrected sentence]\n왜 틀렸을까요?\n[Plain-language grammar explanation. When the rule involves a pattern\n the student will hit again (subject-verb agreement, tense, articles,\n question word order, etc.), show the pattern explicitly — e.g. a short\n person-by-person conjugation comparison (I remain / You remain / She\n remains), or a tense comparison (현재: X / 과거: Y / 진행형: Z) — not\n just \"this is wrong because.\" If a second, smaller error is bundled in\n the same sentence (e.g. singular/plural noun agreement), note it too\n in the same explanation rather than a separate numbered item.]\n\n② [repeat the ①  format for each additional correction]\n(2-3 items, only genuine ones from the transcript — depth over count;\n one well-explained correction beats three shallow ones)\n\n🌟 오늘 [학생]이 정말 잘한 점\n[Quote a specific thing the student produced spontaneously — their own\n attempt at expressing an idea, not just a correct textbook answer.\n Explain concretely why that attempt matters (e.g. reaching for their\n own words instead of reciting the text, using a target word correctly\n in a new context). Acknowledge any grammar issues in the quoted\n attempt honestly, but frame the attempt itself as the win. Close with\n one concrete, small practice suggestion tied to today's actual\n mistakes (e.g. which 1-2 verbs to re-practice at home) rather than\n generic encouragement. 2-4 sentences. If there's a genuine attitude/\n behavior note (child/teen profiles only — see \"Handling attitude/\n behavior issues\" above), fold it in briefly and gently before this\n closing note.]\n```\n\n### Structure — 50-minute class (plain text, no markdown)\n\nSame skeleton, expanded — a 50-minute class covers more ground, so the\nreport should reflect that instead of staying the same length as a\n25-minute one:\n\n```\n📘 [Date] 오늘의 수업 피드백 — [Topic]\n\n[Freshly-written 4-5 sentence summary covering the full arc of the\n longer lesson — e.g. warm-up + main activity + practice, what topic(s)\n were covered and what the student actually produced in each part. Open\n with a specific detail from this class, not a generic scene-setter.\n Addressed per learner profile.]\n\n📝 오늘의 학습 내용\n- [Activity/segment 1 — one line, e.g. \"Unit 핵심 어휘 6개 학습 및 문장 만들기\"]\n- [Activity/segment 2 — one line, e.g. \"지문 읽기 및 소리 내어 읽기\"]\n- [Activity/segment 3 — one line, e.g. \"Reading Comprehension 문제 풀이\"]\n(3-5 lines — the specific things actually done in class, listed as\n sub-activities rather than one generic label)\n\n💬 오늘 배운 주요 표현\n- [Word/Phrase] — [meaning]\n  예문: [student's own example sentence, or the tutor's model if the\n  student didn't produce one]\n  [One specific line on how well the student understood/used it —\n  vary this per item, don't praise every single one identically.]\n(5-7 items — more than the 25-min version since more material was\n covered)\n\n✅ 조금만 다듬으면 더 좋아지는 부분\n[One framing sentence normalizing these as a natural part of learning —\n vary the wording each time.]\n\n① [short label for the error type] : [original] → [corrected]\n❌ [student's original sentence, verbatim from transcript]\n✅ [corrected sentence]\n왜 틀렸을까요?\n[Plain-language grammar explanation. When the rule involves a pattern\n the student will hit again, show it explicitly — a short\n person-by-person conjugation comparison, or a tense comparison\n (현재 / 과거 / 진행형) — not just \"this is wrong because.\" Bundle a\n second smaller error in the same sentence into the same explanation\n rather than a separate numbered item.]\n\n② [repeat the ① format for each additional correction]\n(3-4 items, only genuine ones from the transcript — depth over count)\n\n🌟 오늘 [학생]이 정말 잘한 점\n[Quote a specific thing the student produced spontaneously — their own\n attempt at expressing an idea. Explain concretely why that attempt\n matters. Acknowledge any grammar issues in the quoted attempt\n honestly, but frame the attempt itself as the win. Close with one\n concrete, small practice suggestion tied to today's actual mistakes.\n 3-4 sentences. If there's a genuine attitude/behavior note (child/teen\n profiles only — see \"Handling attitude/behavior issues\" above), fold\n it in briefly and gently before this closing note.]\n```\n\n## Output 2: Tutor Evaluation Report (English, professional)\n\nAudience: the **tutor themselves** (not a third-party head office memo\nabout them) — so address them directly (\"you\"), but this is still a\nfrank performance evaluation, not encouragement. Tone: professional,\nobjective, improvement-focused. Don't soften real issues, but frame them\nas constructive coaching rather than a cold internal memo. No emojis.\n\n### Structure (plain text, no markdown)\n\n```\nTutor Evaluation — [Date] — [Tutor name if known, else \"Tutor\"]\n\n1. Talk Time Ratio (estimated): Tutor [X]% / Student [Y]%\n2. Pacing & Engagement: [on-time start, awkward silences, interruptions,\n   time lost to non-lesson activity, etc. — Pass or Needs Review, with\n   a specific reason]\n3. Teaching Quality: [1-5] / 5\n   - Rationale: [2-3 specific sentences on questioning technique and\n     feedback style, citing what actually happened]\n4. Student Participation: [how actively the student engaged — answered\n   in full sentences vs. one-word answers, initiated topics, asked\n   questions, tried the target language unprompted, etc. Cite specific\n   moments.]\n5. Error Correction: [did the tutor catch and correct errors in the\n   moment, or let them slide? Were corrections clear and did the tutor\n   confirm the student understood? Note any missed correction\n   opportunities.]\n6. Questioning & Interaction: [open-ended vs. closed yes/no questions,\n   follow-up questions, wait time given for the student to answer,\n   whether the tutor built on what the student said.]\n7. Lesson Structure: [how the class was organized — warm-up, main\n   material, wrap-up — and whether transitions were smooth and time was\n   allocated sensibly.]\n8. Strengths: [1-3 specific things the tutor did well, citing what\n   actually happened.]\n9. Areas for Improvement: [1-3 concrete, specific issues — always\n   include at least one, even for a strong lesson, unless the lesson is\n   genuinely flawless.]\n10. Recommended Follow-up Actions: [1-3 concrete next steps for the\n    tutor — what to focus on, adjust, or carry into the next lesson.]\n```\n\nLength scales with class duration too: for a 50-minute class, sections 2,\n3, and 7 can reference multiple segments of the lesson rather than\ntreating it as one continuous block, and sections 8-10 can include up to\n3-4 items instead of 1-3 if the longer class surfaced more to work on.\n\n### Free-talking / warm-up time\n\nDon't penalize a lesson just because less time was spent on the\nworkbook/textbook material. If free-talking or warm-up time was\ngenuinely productive — the student was enjoying it, participating\nactively, and picking up real corrections, new expressions, or\nvocabulary from it — treat that time as equivalent to textbook time in\nsections 3 and 7, and say so explicitly. Only flag it as an issue\n(under Pacing & Engagement / Areas for Improvement) when free-talk time\nwas unproductive filler — small talk that didn't lead anywhere, didn't\nteach anything, or ate into the lesson without the student getting\nvalue from it. The distinction to make in the report is \"productive\nfree-talk\" vs. \"time-wasting,\" not \"textbook\" vs. \"non-textbook.\"\n\n### Estimating Talk Time Ratio\n\nEstimate from transcript turn lengths (word/character count per\nspeaker), not just number of turns. State it's an estimate. A well-run\n1:1 tutoring class typically skews toward more student talk time (e.g.\n40/60 or 35/65 tutor/student) — flag it in section 2 if the tutor is\ndominating the conversation (e.g. 60%+).\n\n### Teaching Quality Score guidance\n\n- 5: Tutor uses open-ended questions, gives specific corrective feedback\n  in the moment, adapts to student's level/interests, natural pacing.\n- 3-4: Solid class, but some missed correction opportunities or somewhat\n  generic questioning, or some pacing/prep issues.\n- 1-2: Minimal engagement, mostly closed yes/no questions, missed\n  obvious errors, awkward pacing/silences.\nDon't default to 5 out of politeness — this report exists to catch real\nissues, so use the full range honestly based on transcript evidence.\n\n## Notes\n\n- If the transcript doesn't give enough signal for a field (e.g. talk\n  time is genuinely ambiguous), say so explicitly rather than inventing\n  a number that looks precise.\n- Never fabricate vocabulary, corrections, or quotes that aren't\n  actually in the transcript.\n- If tutor name/date aren't in the transcript, make a reasonable\n  placeholder (e.g. \"Tutor\", today's date) rather than blocking on it,\n  unless the user is clearly expecting you to ask.";
